      "text": "So I think the old machine learning statement that people say I'm still applies here, which is that the model will only be as good as its training data. And it will reflect that training data. When these models are trained on such massive data sets, it's understandable to see how certain behaviors and biases emerge. So we've seen examples where models confidently produce wrong information, and these are what we call hallucinations, or even worse when they reinforce harmful stereotypes. You know, some platforms have addressed this with many methods. So for example, these things called alignment layers, which allow for human feedback and supervised fine tuning. And these don't actually rewrite the core model, but they sort of nudge the outputs",
